#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153002} :activity/id #uuid "26b78e45-1997-563c-b456-d53ae655f749" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049935 :topic/id #uuid "1fcf3c5f-af9e-5e5e-b7aa-ff9b3a312835" :topic/math-academy-id 2558} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153465 :tutorial/content "The **natural numbers** are the positive whole numbers:\n${\\color{blue}1},\\: {\\color{blue}2},\\: {\\color{blue}3},\\: {\\color{blue}4},\\: {\\color{blue}5},\\ldots$\nWe also refer to them as the **counting numbers**.\n\nThere's some debate among mathematicians about whether zero should be considered a natural number. However, for our purposes, zero is *not* a natural number." :tutorial/id #uuid "ddcec145-4774-553b-836d-76a4a5cd221e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9069 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0fa6983b-4c2c-5789-8e95-585a4e680b9e" :step/math-academy-id 172680 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153003}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152124 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151859 :question/id #uuid "960415f3-fe5d-53ea-a164-5960e800fb3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14642"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0075774-6bd8-54c9-b698-4aacd499fce5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049429}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152871}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Numbers as Natural"} :step/id #uuid "bc685578-51b3-5ffb-b8b8-a5cca744297f" :step/math-academy-id 172681 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153004}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153004 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153466 :tutorial/content "When we extend the natural numbers by joining them with zero and the whole negative numbers, we get the **integers**.\n$\\overbrace{\\ldots, {\\color{red}-5},\\: {\\color{red}-4},\\: {\\color{red}-3},\\: {\\color{red}-2},\\: {\\color{red}-1},\\: {\\color{red}0}, \\: \\!\\underbrace{{\\color{blue}1},\\: {\\color{blue}2}, \\: {\\color{blue}3},\\: {\\color{blue}4},\\: {\\color{blue}5},\\ldots}_{\\large\\textrm{Natural numbers}}}^{\\large\\textrm{Integers}}$\n\nWe can represent the relationship between the natural numbers and integers using an inclusion diagram:\n\n![](images/00/00a2725bbe34eb8bc88f7f6dca6f22e624854a0cb469645eb2f4f9b79a38f846.png)\n\nNotice that the natural numbers are *contained within* the integers. In other words, *every* natural number is also an integer." :tutorial/id #uuid "4109da7f-f9b5-5c94-8215-06f5ea408038" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9070 :tutorial/title "The Integers"} :step/id #uuid "bfd8720b-23e8-55fe-8c02-ac787976007a" :step/math-academy-id 172684 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153005}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153005 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152125 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151860 :question/id #uuid "30b0a464-f903-5ee1-b649-97bfb366a73b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14643"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebf3f930-2768-5399-8470-48ac430065a3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433610}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Numbers as Integers"} :step/id #uuid "34d04639-2557-5679-9ab1-fa22b4733ce3" :step/math-academy-id 172685 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153006}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153467 :tutorial/content "The **rational numbers** consist of all numbers that can be written as the ratio of two integers. For example:\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}-\\dfrac{1}{2}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{3}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}-\\dfrac{2}{3}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nEvery integer is also a rational number because every integer can be expressed as a ratio of two integers. For example,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{black}\\dfrac{2}{1}}, \\qquad {\\color{red}-9} = {\\color{black}\\dfrac{(-9)}{\\phantom{-}1}}\n$$\n\nLikewise, any mixed number is a rational number because it can be written as an improper fraction. For example,\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nis a rational number because\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{2 \\times 3 + 1}{3} = \\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the rational numbers to our inclusion diagram.\n\n![](images/12/124ced6b317bdd2df4f967e6ea45e613b89d9fa136acb5edf8f7ed86fa8a4c06.png)\n\nNotice that the integers (and therefore the natural numbers) are contained within the rational numbers." :tutorial/id #uuid "3563ca37-8d82-5742-92b2-bb3918a71477" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9071 :tutorial/title "The Rational Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "cc715ab1-8b8b-555b-bd73-97d8d33107c0" :step/math-academy-id 172688 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153007}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152126 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151861 :question/id #uuid "471f680f-5a51-5dac-aa3c-834610690f0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14644"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7063140-eebe-598c-b530-3c13509d359b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433608}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Numbers as Rational"} :step/id #uuid "ba79c281-b939-528f-8605-0a6cd2aa6abd" :step/math-academy-id 172689 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153008}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153468 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following rational number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13\n$$\n\nWe can find the decimal representation of this rational number by dividing $1$ by $3$ using long division, as follows:\n\n| $\\,\\,0 \\,. 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3$$\\:\\ldots$$3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$\\,\\ldots$ |\n| --- |\n\nNote that, in this case\n\n- the long division process never ends, and\n- the digit $3$ after the decimal point repeats *forever*.\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13 = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nThis is an example of a **repeating decimal**. A repeating decimal is a decimal where the digits after the decimal point continue forever but in a repeating pattern.\n\nIt's convenient to use the following special notation to indicate decimals that repeat indefinitely.\n\n$$\n0.\\overline{3} = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nSome decimals have two or more digits that repeat indefinitely. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.78^(―) &= 0.78787878… \\\\\n0.675^(―) &= 0.675675675…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSome repeating decimals have non-repeating digits before the repeating part. For example:\n\n$$\n0.333\\overline{67} = 0.333\\,67\\,67\\,67\\,67\\ldots\n$$\n\nThe key takeaway for this section is that every repeating decimal is rational! In other words, every repeating decimal can be expressed as a ratio of two integers.\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.3^(―) &= \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n0.78^(―) &= \\frac{26}{33} \\\\\n0.33367^(―) &= \\frac{16517}{49500}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn a future lesson, we'll learn how to express a repeating decimal as a fraction. For now, you can verify these results using a calculator." :tutorial/id #uuid "28ff4afd-e8c7-511c-86a4-14d547e6c6d2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9072 :tutorial/title "Repeating Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "8cd64bc9-e39e-5f5c-9c0d-618b3294cff3" :step/math-academy-id 172692 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153009}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152127 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151862 :question/id #uuid "f0bf7717-e639-5889-92fe-7cdcce737a55" :question/math-academy-id "e-14645"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be826645-6351-54ab-b57d-2c2a83a1b68d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050724}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152676}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rational Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "7c946365-2b4a-5987-84b7-84cce8c8a6f4" :step/math-academy-id 172693}] :activity/title "Natural Numbers, Integers, and Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152314}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433611} :activity/id #uuid "92c46132-b219-52cf-940d-0d4674e4370a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049935 :topic/id #uuid "1fcf3c5f-af9e-5e5e-b7aa-ff9b3a312835" :topic/math-academy-id 2558} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433607 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "138bd42d-bdd5-5103-a741-25924bfa0d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433583} {:answer/id #uuid "40048675-6488-5fe3-b64d-e1be872b48eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433588} {:answer/id #uuid "739cdf7c-1cac-5a34-beb6-6fa4499d7934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433593} {:answer/id #uuid "899c20e5-4344-5547-829c-68e151ee1feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433596} {:answer/id #uuid "e15793bd-544a-5738-b2d9-b9df1c36295d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433602}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433583} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ddb88e1-f38e-5794-a535-77e7876e74f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "39fe4419-4a43-50fa-a07c-6ab5298bae4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-67819" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\frac{1}{10}$ is natural number\n\nII. $\\frac{1}{10}$ is an integer\n\nIII. $\\frac{1}{10}$ is a rational number" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall our definitions.\n\n- The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers: $1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…$\n\n- The integers consist of all of the positive and negative whole numbers, and zero: $…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$\n\n- The rationals consist of all numbers that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $\\frac{1}{10}$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $\\frac{1}{10}$ is not an integer.\n\n- $\\frac{1}{10}$ is a rational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "44dc1f4c-fd4e-52ad-a63a-2ecc1e1041de" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e712e82-fa0f-5c35-8341-987cad89094f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433584} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5526aa-d152-5495-bbcb-6f1e1ee497c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433591} {:answer/id #uuid "96e7edb9-c054-584a-a80a-d979fedb4496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433597} {:answer/id #uuid "99522998-d655-5aee-9ba1-5f3e13bc3006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433598} {:answer/id #uuid "b0b741c4-7edd-5fb2-9681-7f1843d11f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433599}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433598} :answer-field/id #uuid "189f8c27-4698-52fa-b757-7d2b24c8aa2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eb2e9e07-c134-569e-a208-041621377fa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-99311" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are integers?\n\nI. $100$\n\nII. $-5$\n\nIII. $-\\frac{9}{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The integers consist of all positive and negative whole numbers and zero.\n\n$$\n…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…\n$$\n\n \n\nOf the given numbers, $100$ and $-5$ are integers, but $-\\frac{9}{2}$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "63f2e9a0-ccd8-53e1-823a-f93d8c2355e1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ef60037-3b80-5502-933c-09d72a5ba319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433585} {:answer/id #uuid "20049e96-ae01-5c45-9a3c-1f7838319008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433586} {:answer/id #uuid "32cb23a7-2f0e-5f7f-aacf-0afbcb6147d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433587} {:answer/id #uuid "52d54b01-b14b-5e76-b8dd-55960b680252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433589} {:answer/id #uuid "d85a86a3-e5b3-5d88-8a74-b8f032a1e5b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433601}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433601} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cd1f0fa-8af8-54fb-b456-0eee7ce3fb92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "46c072ed-28fe-5e0e-96d0-a316d330f872" :question/math-academy-id "q-93611" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-10$ is a natural number\n\nII. $-10$ is an integer\n\nIII. $-10$ is a rational number" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall our definitions.\n\n- The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers: $1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…$\n\n- The integers consist of all of the positive and negative whole numbers, and zero: $…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$\n\n- The rationals consist of all numbers that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $-10$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $-10$ is an integer.\n\n- $-10$ is a rational number because it can be written as $\\frac{(-10)}{1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "678e552d-7057-5896-a457-bfa4af875ece" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d28a50a6-7308-5e7d-9506-1e4175511186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151776} {:answer/id #uuid "ed8b1e51-4f39-5465-8987-f4a307226cf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151777} {:answer/id #uuid "171962fe-cdfc-56f5-897b-aa03946cf77e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151778} {:answer/id #uuid "61310617-8554-57e3-9ce9-7002b33a7a58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151779} {:answer/id #uuid "643cedf7-507f-5e63-91cf-0aa5f8a535ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151780}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151780} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd4be8de-607c-5578-91b6-081cf9216fab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c0d528fe-7741-52a6-a947-e2ff7f9a758d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98382" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-2$ is a natural number\n\nII. $-2$ is an integer\n\nIII. $-2$ is a rational number" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall our definitions.\n\n- The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers: $1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…$\n\n- The integers consist of all of the positive and negative whole numbers, and zero: $…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$\n\n- The rationals consist of any number that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $-2$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $-2$ is an integer.\n\n- $-2$ is a rational number because it can be written as $\\frac{(-2)}{1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9656a63a-73b0-53ca-9b94-ede50eec4768" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433612}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57a6471b-bd77-5aee-aeff-e9d5f4387baf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433590} {:answer/id #uuid "66d9b2ee-4fe7-59c8-9132-d913e4e61062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433592} {:answer/id #uuid "75c7099d-8ae5-5ce9-8677-484e3279c83b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433594} {:answer/id #uuid "77a50393-7099-5a74-b6fa-5abc6300ec01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433595} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c8f7ea-0cb0-5b68-9325-dd9c3a2362cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433600}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433595} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e18ef21-c07b-584b-a353-bc2e107acf2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "de7db80c-f818-55cb-b186-c1d21190ea48" :question/math-academy-id "q-98369" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are integers?\n\nI. $\\frac{3}{2}$\n\nII. $-3$\n\nIII. $5$" :question/worked-solution "The integers consist of all positive and negative whole numbers and zero.\n\n$$\n…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…\n$$\n\n \n\nOf the given numbers, $-3$ and $5$ are integers, but $\\frac{3}{2}$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "c7a1ce99-411c-547f-b9eb-715734f5b768" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433614}}] :activity/title "Natural Numbers, Integers, and Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433582}]}
